1
00:00:04,262 --> 00:00:11,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Sankhyātanikaya 3528, Adhīta Sutta, Fire Discourse, or often translated as the Fire Sermon.

2
00:00:13,611 --> 00:00:14,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I heard.

3
00:00:14,972 --> 00:00:18,996
[SPEAKER_00]: One time, the Blessed One was dwelling in Gāyā, at Gāyā's head, with one thousand monks.

4
00:00:19,857 --> 00:00:21,458
[SPEAKER_00]: There, the Blessed One addressed the monks.

5
00:00:21,698 --> 00:00:23,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Monks, all is on fire.

6
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:25,742
[SPEAKER_00]: What monks is the all that is on fire?

7
00:00:26,563 --> 00:00:27,824
[SPEAKER_00]: The I-monks is on fire.

8
00:00:27,944 --> 00:00:28,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Forms are on fire.

9
00:00:29,085 --> 00:00:30,406
[SPEAKER_00]: I-consciousness is on fire.

10
00:00:30,747 --> 00:00:32,748
[SPEAKER_00]: I-impression is on fire.

11
00:00:33,589 --> 00:00:37,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, that which I-impression causes to appear felt pleasant,

12
00:00:37,773 --> 00:00:45,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one thing, like, first thing to note here, it's, eh... things that are on fire, means fire is not in them.

13
00:00:45,038 --> 00:00:49,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you, you, eh... the eye, the forms and eye consciousness, the fire takes them up.

14
00:01:06,012 --> 00:01:08,733
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes them up through passion, through anger, through dullness.

15
00:01:08,973 --> 00:01:13,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that the fire is in the eye or in the forms.

16
00:01:13,754 --> 00:01:25,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason I say this, it's because often, when people would say you have a sight that induces, I don't know, ill-will, anger, so it's on fire with aversion.

17
00:01:26,268 --> 00:01:29,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Hypothetically speaking, you blame the sight.

18
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:37,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Habitually, you sort of jump into the conclusion, into the assumption, that the aversion, the anger, is because of the sight.

19
00:01:37,837 --> 00:01:38,317
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not.

20
00:01:38,497 --> 00:01:40,319
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the anger takes up the sight.

21
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:47,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means that anger, or lust, or dullness, that fire, takes that up as fuel.

22
00:01:47,684 --> 00:01:48,485
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not it.

23
00:01:48,725 --> 00:01:49,446
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in it.

24
00:01:50,335 --> 00:01:56,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then the fuel, or rather, sorry, we know what the fuel is, but what is the reason that the fire takes these things up?

25
00:01:56,678 --> 00:02:02,840
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddha explains the reason is passion, anger and dullness.

26
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Delusion often translated.

27
00:02:04,241 --> 00:02:06,602
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll explain later why we translate it as dullness here.

28
00:02:07,842 --> 00:02:13,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's having passion, which means, ah, so passion is something you're responsible for.

29
00:02:15,048 --> 00:02:17,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Compassion is not in the sight, in the beautiful sights.

30
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:21,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Aversion is not in the in the ugly, disagreeable sights.

31
00:02:22,152 --> 00:02:25,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Dullness, it's not in the neutral sights.

32
00:02:25,774 --> 00:02:29,376
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically something you choose to cultivate in regard to those sights.

33
00:02:29,996 --> 00:02:35,920
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the same, it's exactly the same principle that would be described for the five aggregates and the upādāna.

34
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:42,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five aggregates, pañca khandhā and pañcupādānakkhandhā, said upādāna is not in the five aggregates.

35
00:02:43,144 --> 00:02:45,325
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the assumption is not in the five aggregates.

36
00:02:46,486 --> 00:02:50,128
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates that the assumption is there.

37
00:02:50,148 --> 00:02:58,233
[SPEAKER_00]: An assumption literally means taking things up for granted, assuming them, and that's what fire does, takes them up.

38
00:02:59,702 --> 00:03:10,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not... you don't do... you don't take things up consciously, in a sense of... sorry, not... you don't take things up voluntarily or intentionally, that's the better word.

39
00:03:11,646 --> 00:03:24,530
[SPEAKER_00]: You take them up as a result, or rather taking up is the result of you intentionally cultivating lust, aversion, cultivating passion, aversion, dullness, indolence, and so on.

40
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:27,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you then... that's how it results.

41
00:03:28,221 --> 00:03:30,268
[SPEAKER_00]: That those things become taken up.

42
00:03:30,549 --> 00:03:33,197
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the fire sets in and starts to burn.

43
00:03:34,732 --> 00:03:40,135
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just say no to fire, you can't just say no to the assumptions, you can't say no to upādāna.

44
00:03:40,175 --> 00:03:50,479
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we choose to translate as assumption, not... the word upādāna, we choose to translate as assumption, not as holding or attachment.

45
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:54,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all of those things are something you can be clearly conscious of.

46
00:03:54,701 --> 00:03:58,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm holding on to this, thus now I will choose to let it go.

47
00:03:59,084 --> 00:04:00,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not intentional.

48
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:04,146
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot intentionally choose to let it go, because you're not intentionally holding it.

49
00:04:06,328 --> 00:04:12,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're doing something else, intentionally, that's then directly resulting in upādāna.

50
00:04:13,289 --> 00:04:23,171
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly, like, the fundamental thing that you are responsible for, that is the determining the upādāna in regard to the five aggregates, is ignorance.

51
00:04:24,211 --> 00:04:27,092
[SPEAKER_00]: So, choosing to ignore certain nature of things.

52
00:04:28,032 --> 00:04:28,392
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

53
00:04:28,452 --> 00:04:29,292
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all you need to do.

54
00:04:29,412 --> 00:04:34,813
[SPEAKER_00]: You just choose to turn a blind eye on a certain characteristics of your experience, usually the unpleasant ones.

55
00:04:35,784 --> 00:04:42,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Automatically, that's simultaneously resulting and determining the presence of assumption.

56
00:04:43,947 --> 00:04:47,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you will assume things when you don't know them.

57
00:04:47,828 --> 00:04:50,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't know whether it's like this, so you assume it.

58
00:04:50,388 --> 00:04:52,769
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, you find assumptions there.

59
00:04:53,509 --> 00:04:57,151
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't sort of intentionally create them.

60
00:04:57,271 --> 00:04:59,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, I shall assume so and so.

61
00:04:59,231 --> 00:05:01,512
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you just find yourself assuming so and so.

62
00:05:02,589 --> 00:05:03,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you never questioned it.

63
00:05:03,870 --> 00:05:04,710
[SPEAKER_00]: You never looked at it.

64
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:07,712
[SPEAKER_00]: You were always blind of the nature of that thing.

65
00:05:07,832 --> 00:05:11,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you were assuming it rightly, the fact is you don't know that.

66
00:05:12,355 --> 00:05:15,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason you don't know is because you never checked that assumption.

67
00:05:15,337 --> 00:05:17,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the nature of upādāna.

68
00:05:18,516 --> 00:05:24,918
[SPEAKER_00]: The assumption in regard to the form, feeling, perception, intentions and consciousness.

69
00:05:25,838 --> 00:05:32,340
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not this some great mystery of the aggregates that you need to reveal and discover.

70
00:05:32,940 --> 00:05:37,182
[SPEAKER_00]: It's simply ignoring certain fundamental characteristics of the five aggregates.

71
00:05:37,242 --> 00:05:38,162
[SPEAKER_00]: The assumption is there.

72
00:05:39,282 --> 00:05:54,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ignoring the certain characteristics of the five aggregates, or ignoring the nature of the senses, means passion, aversion, dullness in regard to them, are the attitudes you choose to cultivate.

73
00:05:55,490 --> 00:06:01,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, they are on fire because of what you do underneath, so to speak.

74
00:06:01,993 --> 00:06:05,295
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you see a beautiful sight and you have passion in regard to it,

75
00:06:06,289 --> 00:06:16,392
[SPEAKER_00]: First, you have to stop blaming the beautiful sight for the passion, have to take responsibility for the passion, and the only way to do that is to restrain acting out of that passion, habitually, straight away towards the sight.

76
00:06:17,233 --> 00:06:27,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you restrain, once you feel the burning of the fire, the burning of the passion, or aversion, or even delusion, you get to realize, okay, so it's... it's underneath this.

77
00:06:28,116 --> 00:06:33,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Fire is something that I do, which results in me basically being liable to burn.

78
00:06:35,462 --> 00:06:40,280
[SPEAKER_00]: When the sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches create impressions on me.

79
00:06:42,543 --> 00:06:57,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And that thing that's underneath is what the Buddha described with certain subtle attitudes of delighting in sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, welcoming sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, adhering to them, hoping for...

80
00:06:57,330 --> 00:07:06,735
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all these little attitudes that you cultivate towards accepting the pleasant ones and rejecting the unpleasant ones, and you just assume that

81
00:07:08,108 --> 00:07:09,349
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fine to do so.

82
00:07:09,469 --> 00:07:11,790
[SPEAKER_00]: You assume that you're in a position to do so.

83
00:07:12,851 --> 00:07:18,615
[SPEAKER_00]: You assume that it's that it's absolutely normal to when there is pleasant, you just go towards it.

84
00:07:19,375 --> 00:07:21,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it's unpleasant, you go away from it.

85
00:07:21,357 --> 00:07:24,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it's neutral, you just sort of be blind of it.

86
00:07:24,919 --> 00:07:28,762
[SPEAKER_00]: be a bit dull and indolent in regard to it.

87
00:07:29,223 --> 00:07:32,565
[SPEAKER_00]: And that assumption is the direct cause, then, that these things are on fire.

88
00:07:33,286 --> 00:07:34,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring the nature of those things.

89
00:07:34,647 --> 00:07:41,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as the Buddha said, so how do you... how would you stop assuming that it's fine to adhere to these things, to reject these things?

90
00:07:42,513 --> 00:07:48,798
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you stop delighting in sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, or having... being averse?

91
00:07:49,179 --> 00:07:50,680
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you stop those attitudes?

92
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:57,265
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would stop them by seeing the danger in doing so.

93
00:07:58,086 --> 00:08:06,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there is actually danger in accepting the beautiful sight, in delighting in it, in being elated on account of it.

94
00:08:07,758 --> 00:08:09,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the danger is not in the sight.

95
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:11,302
[SPEAKER_00]: The sight is not dangerous.

96
00:08:12,790 --> 00:08:14,851
[SPEAKER_00]: The eye is not dangerous.

97
00:08:15,091 --> 00:08:17,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the fire is not dangerous, so to speak.

98
00:08:17,071 --> 00:08:18,312
[SPEAKER_00]: The fire is just a symptom.

99
00:08:18,352 --> 00:08:20,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that are on fire means, oh, they're burning.

100
00:08:20,612 --> 00:08:23,173
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a symptom of that underlying fuel.

101
00:08:25,094 --> 00:08:27,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is the danger of that fuel?

102
00:08:27,234 --> 00:08:37,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the danger is in basically not seeing that welcoming, delighting, adhering, being elated on account of it.

103
00:08:37,497 --> 00:08:39,018
[SPEAKER_00]: That attitude is dangerous.

104
00:08:40,023 --> 00:08:41,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And why is it dangerous?

105
00:08:42,064 --> 00:08:43,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Naturally, you would ask.

106
00:08:44,064 --> 00:08:47,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because that which you delight in, is not in your control.

107
00:08:48,365 --> 00:08:56,107
[SPEAKER_00]: That which you're welcoming, hoping to last, hoping to come your way, and persist, and stay, and not leave you, is not in your control.

108
00:08:56,707 --> 00:09:07,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you delight in sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, without simultaneously ignoring the fact that sight, sound, smell, taste, touches are not in your control?

109
00:09:08,964 --> 00:09:11,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, literally, inaccessible to you.

110
00:09:11,765 --> 00:09:13,386
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, like, oh, not in my control, but no.

111
00:09:13,646 --> 00:09:14,547
[SPEAKER_00]: They're inaccessible.

112
00:09:14,567 --> 00:09:16,028
[SPEAKER_00]: They have life of their own.

113
00:09:17,668 --> 00:09:19,529
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't even know about you, so to speak.

114
00:09:19,829 --> 00:09:20,910
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't exist for them.

115
00:09:21,030 --> 00:09:28,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are the one picking these things up with all this, like, emotional, eh... craving for pleasure and so on.

116
00:09:29,114 --> 00:09:34,377
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would you be able to delight to welcome these things without

117
00:09:35,704 --> 00:09:38,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneously ignoring that they're not in your control.

118
00:09:38,686 --> 00:09:47,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ignoring that these things are not in your control is a necessary basis for being able to delight, rejoice, be elated, welcome it, adhere to it.

119
00:09:48,850 --> 00:09:55,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, ah, not ignoring that these things are not in your control, means you will be unable to delight in them.

120
00:09:56,874 --> 00:09:59,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean you won't see things with your eye.

121
00:09:59,436 --> 00:10:03,237
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't recognize whether they're beautiful, agreeable or ugly and disagreeable.

122
00:10:03,257 --> 00:10:04,218
[SPEAKER_00]: That all stays the same.

123
00:10:05,110 --> 00:10:15,052
[SPEAKER_00]: It just means now you're not... you're being unable to ignore the nature, the context in which they appear, such as they're not in your control.

124
00:10:15,452 --> 00:10:16,212
[SPEAKER_00]: They're given to you.

125
00:10:16,272 --> 00:10:17,512
[SPEAKER_00]: You are subjected to them.

126
00:10:18,032 --> 00:10:19,552
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no say in their arising.

127
00:10:19,632 --> 00:10:34,095
[SPEAKER_00]: All these things that, again, you can think about theoretically and abstractly, but that won't work in the same sense if you think of it on the level of the actual, currently enduring sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts, the mind.

128
00:10:35,955 --> 00:10:39,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, that's why we translate passa as pressure and impression.

129
00:10:39,916 --> 00:10:44,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Because these things arise on their own, and you run... they exercise pressure on you automatically.

130
00:10:44,598 --> 00:10:51,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure, like pleasure, exercises pressure to, to, eh... to go towards it, to have more of it.

131
00:10:53,261 --> 00:10:56,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Pain exercises pressure to get away from it.

132
00:10:56,522 --> 00:10:58,903
[SPEAKER_00]: It pressures you.

133
00:10:58,923 --> 00:11:02,684
[SPEAKER_00]: And neutral feeling exercises pressure towards, basically, ignoring.

134
00:11:03,883 --> 00:11:09,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Being dull, mentally, sort of, unaware, unmindful, and so on.

135
00:11:09,767 --> 00:11:11,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Not keeping that mental alertness.

136
00:11:11,909 --> 00:11:15,231
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what neutral feeling pressures you towards, towards that dullness.

137
00:11:15,851 --> 00:11:17,132
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we translate it as dullness.

138
00:11:17,152 --> 00:11:21,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Because delusion, it's sort of, again, it has certain connotations of this

139
00:11:23,566 --> 00:11:24,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Something you're deluded about.

140
00:11:25,268 --> 00:11:26,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Until you figure it out, you'll be deluded.

141
00:11:26,909 --> 00:11:27,009
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

142
00:11:27,029 --> 00:11:28,590
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more of an attitude of the mind.

143
00:11:28,630 --> 00:11:32,273
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, passion is an attitude towards pleasure.

144
00:11:32,593 --> 00:11:35,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Aversion is an attitude away from pain.

145
00:11:35,516 --> 00:11:45,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Dullness is the attitude towards that neutral feeling that's kind of undistinguishable, really, by any means of pleasure or pain.

146
00:11:47,105 --> 00:11:47,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

147
00:11:48,888 --> 00:12:07,174
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why recognition, then, that these things are not in your control, they're there of the nature to cease having a reason, they're impermanent, you cannot basically have them submit to your will, it's the other way around, will then make you not delight in them.

148
00:12:09,594 --> 00:12:24,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you know there is a beautiful sight, and if you know any second can be taken away, any second it can sort of intoxicate you and completely make you spiral out of control, for example, you would regard that thing as dangerous.

149
00:12:24,270 --> 00:12:25,251
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't go near it.

150
00:12:26,873 --> 00:12:31,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by regarding that, by seeing the danger in these things, the Buddha said, you will stop delighting.

151
00:12:32,139 --> 00:12:33,200
[SPEAKER_00]: You will stop welcoming it.

152
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:40,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, when there is aversion, you also delight in your own attitude of trying to get rid of that aversion.

153
00:12:42,904 --> 00:12:44,945
[SPEAKER_00]: You also accept that attitude for granted.

154
00:12:46,517 --> 00:12:52,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you see that aversion has arisen on its own, it means, oh, it's uncomfortable, but I'm not saying it.

155
00:12:52,842 --> 00:12:54,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Having arisen, it will have to cease.

156
00:12:54,563 --> 00:12:56,505
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just it's impossible for it to not cease.

157
00:12:57,205 --> 00:13:03,550
[SPEAKER_00]: That's enough for you to not be pressured by that aversion, if you maintain that context, especially if you clarify it further and further.

158
00:13:04,571 --> 00:13:09,455
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by seeing the danger in the eye, seeing the danger in the forms,

159
00:13:11,278 --> 00:13:12,940
[SPEAKER_00]: And same goes for all the other senses.

160
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:23,169
[SPEAKER_00]: You will stop delighting in it, you will stop being averse towards it, you will stop resisting it, and you will stop giving in to that mental indolence and dullness.

161
00:13:23,309 --> 00:13:32,598
[SPEAKER_00]: By stopping of those attitudes and those choices, you then remove that fuel for the fire.

162
00:13:33,139 --> 00:13:34,340
[SPEAKER_00]: So the fire will diminish.

163
00:13:38,637 --> 00:13:46,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you would see the same sight that used to fill your mind with passion or fill your mind with anger, that you'll be burning with, now there is no fire.

164
00:13:47,447 --> 00:13:51,833
[SPEAKER_00]: The sight hasn't changed, the eyes haven't changed, the consciousness of that hasn't changed.

165
00:13:52,213 --> 00:13:53,695
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still aware of the same things.

166
00:13:55,167 --> 00:14:00,791
[SPEAKER_00]: What has changed now, is there is no necessary basis of your delighting in those things.

167
00:14:00,831 --> 00:14:03,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, there is no fire being manifested.

168
00:14:03,954 --> 00:14:12,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Because fire needs the delight to... in order... fire needs the delight, as in fire needs passion, aversion, dullness, for it to burn.

169
00:14:13,962 --> 00:14:15,603
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to... it needs to fuel it.

170
00:14:19,110 --> 00:14:20,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the eye is on fire.

171
00:14:20,732 --> 00:14:31,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, that attitude of fire then takes any... whichever way you look, say, on the experience of, like, a disagreeable sight, something fills you... you've seen something that fills you with aversion.

172
00:14:32,362 --> 00:14:45,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... the fire is there because of the aversion, not because of the eye, not because of the sight, not because of you being aware of the eye and the sight, not even because of the pressure that that sight exhibits on you.

173
00:14:46,818 --> 00:14:56,383
[SPEAKER_00]: The fire is there because of desire, because of passion, because of aversion, because of delusion, because of dullness, whichever way you want to translate it.

174
00:14:57,803 --> 00:15:04,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why, then, that fire takes up anything within that relationship, within the domain of sight.

175
00:15:05,247 --> 00:15:10,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the eye will be on fire, the sights will be on fire, everything in between will be on fire.

176
00:15:12,047 --> 00:15:13,448
[SPEAKER_00]: So, high consciousness will be on fire.

177
00:15:13,488 --> 00:15:18,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure that that experience exhibits on you, impression created by that sight, will be on fire.

178
00:15:21,791 --> 00:15:28,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything that's felt on account of whatever you've seen, pleasant, unpleasant, neither unpleasant or pleasant, that's all on fire.

179
00:15:30,314 --> 00:15:37,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what this states is that, through your unchecked delight, welcoming, assuming,

180
00:15:38,442 --> 00:15:44,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Not seeing the nature of the sight, sound, smell, taste, touch is not having reflected upon it sufficiently enough.

181
00:15:45,395 --> 00:15:50,496
[SPEAKER_00]: You have accumulated the abundance of fire, let's say like that.

182
00:15:51,096 --> 00:15:54,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Which now means that this fire is rolling.

183
00:15:54,177 --> 00:15:57,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, once the big fire starts, it starts taking things up on its own.

184
00:15:58,558 --> 00:16:00,339
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it becomes kind of self-sufficient.

185
00:16:00,379 --> 00:16:03,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, for as long as there is eye, sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

186
00:16:03,899 --> 00:16:07,260
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as there are things to be taken up by fire, fire will take it up.

187
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:15,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the only way to diminish that fire, it's not enough for you to just nod delight in these things once, twice, three times.

188
00:16:15,402 --> 00:16:32,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Thousand times, you have to develop the attitude of perpetual non-delight, of perpetual non-resistance to pain, non-acceptance of pleasure, and not slipping into dullness on account of neutral feeling.

189
00:16:33,255 --> 00:16:36,137
[SPEAKER_00]: And what will constitute that perpetual attitude?

190
00:16:36,157 --> 00:16:37,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that depends on the individual.

191
00:16:37,738 --> 00:16:41,160
[SPEAKER_00]: For somebody, that might be done in a shorter period of time.

192
00:16:41,280 --> 00:16:42,061
[SPEAKER_00]: For somebody, longer.

193
00:16:42,121 --> 00:16:44,563
[SPEAKER_00]: But on average, you would assume it will take quite a while

194
00:16:45,888 --> 00:16:54,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it took quite a while to accumulate that massive fire through your unchecked desires, passions, aversions and so on, things you've been acting out of.

195
00:16:55,933 --> 00:17:02,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have developed that attitude,

196
00:17:04,053 --> 00:17:18,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Of non-giving in to resistance to pain, acceptance of pleasure, and then acting out of it, you have basically, gradually, started removing things.

197
00:17:18,665 --> 00:17:26,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just directly go head-on and extinguish the fire, but you can start removing that which fire can take up as fuel.

198
00:17:27,552 --> 00:17:29,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And eventually, it will have to burn out.

199
00:17:30,965 --> 00:17:35,818
[SPEAKER_00]: And it will burn out having not taking anything new as fuel.

200
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:37,984
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when the fire is extinguished.

201
00:17:39,230 --> 00:17:41,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where, literally, there's nothing else then to do.

202
00:17:41,791 --> 00:17:47,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, the eye, the sights, the nose, the smells, and so on, the mind, the thoughts remain.

203
00:17:48,273 --> 00:17:50,694
[SPEAKER_00]: And with that said, those things remain in Tathāgata as well.

204
00:17:51,274 --> 00:17:57,836
[SPEAKER_00]: And the beautiful, recognizing beautiful things, recognizing ugly things, all that Tathāgata still has.

205
00:17:58,296 --> 00:18:00,617
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't lost preferences or anything like that.

206
00:18:01,357 --> 00:18:04,378
[SPEAKER_00]: What Tathāgata doesn't have is the desire and lust

207
00:18:04,758 --> 00:18:10,331
[SPEAKER_00]: in regard to his own thought, in regard to his perceptions, in regard to his sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and so on.

208
00:18:11,428 --> 00:18:26,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by practicing, by discerning the danger in these things, you will prevent yourself from habitually choosing passion, choosing aversion, choosing dullness, giving in, cultivating, dwelling in it.

209
00:18:27,558 --> 00:18:29,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You will prevent yourself from doing it.

210
00:18:29,139 --> 00:18:34,582
[SPEAKER_00]: By preventing yourself from doing it, you are removing the basis for fire to keep finding fuel.

211
00:18:35,697 --> 00:18:42,241
[SPEAKER_00]: So, indirectly speaking, then, you are extinguishing the fire, which is kind of, in a way, a direct way of doing it.

212
00:18:42,862 --> 00:18:50,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Removing the next thing the fire will take up, you know it will directly cause that fire to burn out, to stop, to disappear.

213
00:18:52,908 --> 00:19:01,834
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's important to recognize here that the fire is on the basis of your attitude of passion, anger and dullness.

214
00:19:03,561 --> 00:19:05,383
[SPEAKER_00]: ill-will, aversion and dullness.

215
00:19:05,523 --> 00:19:09,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in the eye, in the sight, in the consciousness and so on.

216
00:19:13,592 --> 00:19:16,976
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the same goes, the sutta carries on for all the other senses, including the mind.

217
00:19:17,076 --> 00:19:23,864
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind is on fire, phenomena are on fire, mind consciousness is on fire, mind impression is on fire.

218
00:19:26,407 --> 00:19:33,556
[SPEAKER_00]: That which mind impression causes to appear, felt pleasant or painful, neither painful nor pleasant, that is also on fire.

219
00:19:33,656 --> 00:19:34,337
[SPEAKER_00]: On fire with what?

220
00:19:34,457 --> 00:19:40,525
[SPEAKER_00]: On fire with passion, with anger, with dullness, with birth, aging, death, subtle limitation, pain, grief and despair.

221
00:19:41,006 --> 00:19:41,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this carries on.

222
00:19:41,887 --> 00:19:43,129
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just piling up.

223
00:19:43,329 --> 00:19:43,890
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it is.

224
00:19:44,845 --> 00:19:53,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's on fire with passion, with anger, with dullness, because you delight in it, you don't see danger in it, which then means you... on account of that fire, it's on fire with birth, aging and death.

225
00:19:53,947 --> 00:19:59,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Means birth, aging, death, all these things that are affecting sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, mind and so on.

226
00:20:00,849 --> 00:20:01,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, they pertain to you.

227
00:20:01,790 --> 00:20:06,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, they are exhibiting pressure on you.

228
00:20:06,291 --> 00:20:07,391
[SPEAKER_00]: You are liable to them.

229
00:20:07,471 --> 00:20:08,671
[SPEAKER_00]: You are subject to them.

230
00:20:09,532 --> 00:20:10,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of, eh...

231
00:20:12,175 --> 00:20:19,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Accepting things that are born, that are aging, that are dying.

232
00:20:19,963 --> 00:20:21,524
[SPEAKER_00]: The body, the senses, and so on.

233
00:20:23,246 --> 00:20:26,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Another layer then, on piling up, is sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief, despair.

234
00:20:26,449 --> 00:20:35,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means any sorrow, any lamentation, any pain, any grief, any despair, on account of anything in this life, it's not specific.

235
00:20:37,027 --> 00:20:51,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Any sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair is because you make yourself liable to it, because you make yourself... because you cultivate passion, anger and dullness in regard to completely unrelated things.

236
00:20:52,818 --> 00:20:58,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be passionate about one thing and then suffer on account of something completely, seemingly unrelated.

237
00:20:58,782 --> 00:21:03,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because there is passion present in experience, you're liable to suffering on account of anything.

238
00:21:05,618 --> 00:21:12,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's hard to approve these things, and it takes a constant repetition, because you cannot see a direct connection.

239
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:20,882
[SPEAKER_00]: If you could immediately see things you're passionate about, how the pain can result there, right away, written immediately, it would be easy to restrain yourself.

240
00:21:22,443 --> 00:21:23,843
[SPEAKER_00]: But that connection is covered up.

241
00:21:24,663 --> 00:21:31,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just rejoicing in a beautiful sight of a familiar person, or a person you love, or something like that.

242
00:21:32,706 --> 00:21:33,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, why is that bad?

243
00:21:34,701 --> 00:21:48,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in itself it's not bad, but having an underlying attitude of passion towards that feeling you experience on account of that sight, that's mean, those impressions, whatever they cause to appear, that's felt pleasant, unpleasant or neutral,

244
00:21:50,330 --> 00:21:51,251
[SPEAKER_00]: You delight in that.

245
00:21:51,871 --> 00:21:53,233
[SPEAKER_00]: You have passion towards that feeling.

246
00:21:53,253 --> 00:21:56,396
[SPEAKER_00]: You have anger towards the unpleasant feeling, or dullness towards neutral feeling.

247
00:21:57,096 --> 00:22:06,585
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, because the passion is present, or anger, or dullness, you are then liable to any type of suffering when things change.

248
00:22:07,948 --> 00:22:14,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as the Buddha would say, being united with things you don't want, and separated from things you want.

249
00:22:14,753 --> 00:22:21,339
[SPEAKER_00]: In brief, the five aggregates of your experience, like your experience as a whole, being affected by assumption.

250
00:22:22,039 --> 00:22:33,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming yes, assuming no, assuming neither yes nor no, giving in to those attitudes, not being mindful about them, and basically cultivating passion, aversion, and dullness in regard to them.

251
00:22:34,069 --> 00:22:35,230
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you are liable to

252
00:22:35,810 --> 00:22:47,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair, absolutely regardless of specific things that might cause, that might trigger, let's say like that, those unpleasant states.

253
00:22:47,958 --> 00:22:56,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the ultimate understanding of the Four Noble Truths is that suffering is determined by craving, not by anything else.

254
00:22:57,448 --> 00:23:04,133
[SPEAKER_00]: So, presence of craving in your experience, which is manifested through passion, manifested through anger, manifested through aversion.

255
00:23:04,853 --> 00:23:07,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering.

256
00:23:07,675 --> 00:23:10,837
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like the two sides of the same coin.

257
00:23:11,017 --> 00:23:14,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving is one side, suffering is the other side.

258
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:16,581
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a separate coin.

259
00:23:17,722 --> 00:23:20,283
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not craving arises first and then suffering follows.

260
00:23:20,844 --> 00:23:27,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of craving means presence of suffering, even if you don't see it, because you're looking on this coin side of craving.

261
00:23:28,152 --> 00:23:30,713
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you get to know that suffering is there.

262
00:23:30,733 --> 00:23:38,435
[SPEAKER_00]: The liability to suffering, which is suffering in itself, is there because of craving, not because you've seen something disagreeable and so on.

263
00:23:39,835 --> 00:23:42,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, craving equals suffering.

264
00:23:42,115 --> 00:23:43,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving means suffering.

265
00:23:45,436 --> 00:23:49,937
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you could also say, in the relation to this sutta, craving means fire.

266
00:23:51,398 --> 00:23:55,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have the attitude of craving on account of feeling that's impressed upon you,

267
00:23:57,588 --> 00:24:07,571
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the attitude of either passion, either anger, or either dullness, three modes through which craving manifests itself, or... and perpetuates itself.

268
00:24:09,011 --> 00:24:15,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of craving, you are then sorrowing, lamenting, in pain, in grieving, sooner or later.

269
00:24:16,054 --> 00:24:16,994
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove craving,

270
00:24:17,801 --> 00:24:21,962
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot sorrow, lament, pain, nor be grieving, nor be desperate, nor kind of anything.

271
00:24:22,462 --> 00:24:25,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's the worst possible misfortune comes your way.

272
00:24:26,223 --> 00:24:32,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Because those things cannot be the reason for the presence of suffering.

273
00:24:32,065 --> 00:24:36,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Those things can then just be taken up by a fire, by passion, by suffering.

274
00:24:37,946 --> 00:24:39,307
[SPEAKER_00]: But the suffering is not in there.

275
00:24:41,446 --> 00:24:44,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the Buddha means here, furthermore, in that sutta.

276
00:24:44,927 --> 00:24:56,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying thus, monks, well-learned, noble disciple, means at least a sotāpanna, means one who has developed the right view that, ah, craving means suffering, not the sights that I've seen that's unpleasant.

277
00:24:57,351 --> 00:25:01,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving means suffering, not the sights that I've seen that cause passion in me and so on.

278
00:25:02,472 --> 00:25:09,978
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Four Noble Truths, it's basically one insight, which means presence of craving equals presence of suffering.

279
00:25:10,198 --> 00:25:15,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of craving, absence of suffering, even if the circumstances haven't changed, if it's all the same.

280
00:25:17,003 --> 00:25:23,108
[SPEAKER_00]: The only thing that changes is absence of craving, means you will not suffer, even if it's the circumstances that used to make you suffer.

281
00:25:24,429 --> 00:25:29,073
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having understood that, you then become wary of the I.

282
00:25:30,319 --> 00:25:32,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Wary of the forms and eye consciousness.

283
00:25:32,380 --> 00:25:34,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wary of the domain of sight.

284
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:51,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Any impression the sights can have on you, you realize, well, anything I see, hear, smell, taste, touch, it exercises pressure on me, which, if I am not mindful of, let's say like that, means I will give in to craving.

285
00:25:51,605 --> 00:25:52,745
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll basically carelessly.

286
00:25:54,123 --> 00:26:02,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I better not cultivate that careless attitude towards the I-forms, I-consciousness and I-impression.

287
00:26:02,829 --> 00:26:13,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being wary of the I, wary of the forms, not because I and forms are to blame, but because, well, basically, you're an addict of passion, addict of aversion, addict of dullness.

288
00:26:13,676 --> 00:26:21,582
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that addiction is in you, but in order to start uprooting it, you need to be wary of those things that you go for.

289
00:26:22,747 --> 00:26:24,848
[SPEAKER_00]: And in this case, it's the sights in general.

290
00:26:25,048 --> 00:26:25,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Not this.

291
00:26:25,648 --> 00:26:28,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Like in the beginning, yeah, you restrain this sight on account of that sight.

292
00:26:28,930 --> 00:26:37,014
[SPEAKER_00]: But eventually, with your reflection and contemplation and meditation, you arrive at any sight means any pressure means any possibility of craving.

293
00:26:37,594 --> 00:26:39,135
[SPEAKER_00]: So you want to be wary of it.

294
00:26:39,675 --> 00:26:41,876
[SPEAKER_00]: And in other words, you want to stop welcoming it.

295
00:26:42,997 --> 00:26:44,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop delighting in it.

296
00:26:45,658 --> 00:26:48,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop looking forward to the prospect of pleasing sights.

297
00:26:49,121 --> 00:26:52,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop looking forward to the prospect of avoiding the displeasing sights.

298
00:26:53,584 --> 00:27:02,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what it means you become wary of the eye, the forms, the eye consciousness, and anything that those things... any way those things can impress you.

299
00:27:04,953 --> 00:27:13,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's whatever's felt on account of it, pleasant, painful, neither pleasant nor... neither painful nor pleasant, you become wary of.

300
00:27:13,040 --> 00:27:14,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wary of whatever you're currently feeling.

301
00:27:18,012 --> 00:27:19,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Worry of whatever you're currently feeling.

302
00:27:20,194 --> 00:27:25,979
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are, say, wary of the present enduring feeling of pleasure, let's say.

303
00:27:26,139 --> 00:27:27,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, pleasure is there.

304
00:27:27,020 --> 00:27:28,541
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not trying to deny, trying to get rid of it.

305
00:27:28,561 --> 00:27:29,301
[SPEAKER_00]: You're wary of it.

306
00:27:30,462 --> 00:27:37,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be able to blindly, carelessly, just crave for it and act out of that craving and give in to desire and lose your sense restraint?

307
00:27:37,308 --> 00:27:37,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

308
00:27:39,430 --> 00:27:41,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through being wary of the I,

309
00:27:42,292 --> 00:27:54,177
[SPEAKER_00]: The forms, the experience of the sight, the eye consciousness, the pressure that's felt on account of it... Oh, now you're wary of how you feel on account of it, which means you're automatically wary of all these things.

310
00:27:55,197 --> 00:27:57,538
[SPEAKER_00]: And by being wary of it, you will not give in to craving.

311
00:27:57,578 --> 00:28:02,240
[SPEAKER_00]: By not giving in to craving, you cannot then perpetuate passion, aversion and dullness.

312
00:28:03,480 --> 00:28:06,842
[SPEAKER_00]: And without those things, fire cannot spread itself.

313
00:28:06,882 --> 00:28:07,762
[SPEAKER_00]: It runs out the fuel.

314
00:28:08,924 --> 00:28:18,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you're wary of the sight, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, the point is to bring you back to be wary of whatever's presently enduring on the level of the feeling, whatever's currently felt.

315
00:28:18,907 --> 00:28:24,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Either pleasant, or if it's felt unpleasantly, or neither pleasantly nor unpleasantly, you're wary of that.

316
00:28:24,668 --> 00:28:27,269
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're wary of that, you cannot crave towards any of that feeling.

317
00:28:27,289 --> 00:28:28,769
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot lean towards pleasant.

318
00:28:29,249 --> 00:28:31,330
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot lean away towards unpleasant feeling.

319
00:28:31,890 --> 00:28:36,011
[SPEAKER_00]: And you cannot, sort of, lean into indolence and dullness on account of neutral feeling.

320
00:28:37,105 --> 00:28:48,714
[SPEAKER_00]: So, simply by understanding where the problem is, which is what the right view is, you then get to exercise, so to speak, these attitudes correctly.

321
00:28:48,774 --> 00:28:53,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the pleasant feeling, you get to exercise the non-leaning in the pleasant feeling.

322
00:28:54,438 --> 00:29:00,503
[SPEAKER_00]: In the unpleasant feeling, by being wary of the unpleasant feeling, you don't automatically lean away from it.

323
00:29:00,523 --> 00:29:05,627
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start exercising non... or rather the absence of leaning away from it.

324
00:29:07,393 --> 00:29:09,634
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha meant in that... in the verse.

325
00:29:10,674 --> 00:29:15,256
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, like, oh, if you... if you kind of fight the waves, if you go... if you go against them, you'll sink.

326
00:29:15,376 --> 00:29:17,837
[SPEAKER_00]: If you try to go backwards, it will... it will...

327
00:29:19,158 --> 00:29:20,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you fight the waves, you get tired.

328
00:29:21,039 --> 00:29:24,320
[SPEAKER_00]: If you go backwards, you get swept away.

329
00:29:24,780 --> 00:29:26,941
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you try and stand still, you... you would sink.

330
00:29:26,981 --> 00:29:30,003
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how did you then cross the... the... to the other shore, they asked the Buddha.

331
00:29:30,023 --> 00:29:35,405
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, well, I crossed it by not fighting the waves, by not pulling backwards and by not standing still.

332
00:29:37,177 --> 00:29:52,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by not leaning towards pleasant feeling, by not leaning away towards unpleasant feeling, and by not being sort of sinking into dullness towards neutral feeling, that's how you will remove the passion, aversion and delusion.

333
00:29:53,753 --> 00:29:56,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point there is that you need to...

334
00:29:58,077 --> 00:30:00,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, oh, I will never lean towards anything.

335
00:30:02,169 --> 00:30:03,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't lean towards anything.

336
00:30:03,753 --> 00:30:08,827
[SPEAKER_00]: You only lean, on that emotional level, towards things that are agreeable.

337
00:30:10,255 --> 00:30:18,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you don't lean towards things that are agreeable, but then you should maybe lean towards things that are disagreeable, because habitually you're leaning away.

338
00:30:19,397 --> 00:30:23,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So, simply by leaning back towards the disagreeable, you stop leaning away.

339
00:30:23,238 --> 00:30:31,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, yeah, okay, you can then double down and start leaning, like self-mortification, but that's when you don't have the context of to what extent it's necessary.

340
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:33,900
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why this applies to the noble disciple.

341
00:30:34,661 --> 00:30:36,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you know that you lean only

342
00:30:37,522 --> 00:30:38,903
[SPEAKER_00]: for the disappearance of craving.

343
00:30:39,063 --> 00:30:41,964
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, craving means leaning towards pleasure.

344
00:30:42,504 --> 00:30:43,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving is there.

345
00:30:43,885 --> 00:30:45,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Leaning away from pain, craving is there.

346
00:30:46,886 --> 00:30:51,569
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're leaning towards pleasure, lean away sufficiently enough for craving to disappear.

347
00:30:51,769 --> 00:30:52,169
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

348
00:30:53,523 --> 00:31:01,345
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't have that as a criteria, the disappearance of craving, is the measure where you stop leaning away from pleasure, you then lean away too much.

349
00:31:01,906 --> 00:31:08,588
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the simile... we mention that often, that... the deer herd, the simile of bait, sotā, in Madhyamanikāya.

350
00:31:09,448 --> 00:31:16,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is when the deer herd realizes, oh, this pleasure, sense pleasures, are basically a trap.

351
00:31:17,072 --> 00:31:17,693
[SPEAKER_00]: They're a bait.

352
00:31:18,233 --> 00:31:19,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, they shun it.

353
00:31:19,153 --> 00:31:28,919
[SPEAKER_00]: They lean away, not just from the bait, but they lean away from anything agreeable, anything that comes from the domain where the bait is, which is why, then, they starve.

354
00:31:29,439 --> 00:31:30,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the other one is sort of

355
00:31:31,465 --> 00:31:32,945
[SPEAKER_00]: You're completely careless about it.

356
00:31:33,005 --> 00:31:34,986
[SPEAKER_00]: One is sort of denying it too much.

357
00:31:35,006 --> 00:31:36,246
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I'm describing here.

358
00:31:36,266 --> 00:31:40,227
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the attitude of habitually just having an experience of pleasant feeling.

359
00:31:41,727 --> 00:31:42,727
[SPEAKER_00]: You're leaning towards it.

360
00:31:43,428 --> 00:31:46,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will lean towards it if you're not wary of that feeling.

361
00:31:47,449 --> 00:31:51,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, becoming wary of the feeling, you stop leaning towards it habitually.

362
00:31:51,909 --> 00:31:53,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes, you might lean away from it.

363
00:31:54,843 --> 00:31:56,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Compensate for the amount you were leaning forward.

364
00:31:57,724 --> 00:31:59,785
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, same in the unpleasant feeling.

365
00:31:59,845 --> 00:32:09,389
[SPEAKER_00]: You habitually in a way, which means sometimes you might need to lean forward, just to regain the perspective on that unpleasant feeling and counter the habitual leaning away.

366
00:32:10,858 --> 00:32:16,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't use it as a rule of thumb that I will always lean away from unpleasant feeling.

367
00:32:16,622 --> 00:32:22,185
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no reason to lean away from pleasant feeling if you haven't been leaning towards.

368
00:32:22,705 --> 00:32:25,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes you don't lean towards pleasure even when you see it.

369
00:32:28,239 --> 00:32:38,787
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just take it as a rule of thumb, that you will always lean away from pleasure, and always lean towards pain, to counter your craving, means you're doing it blindly.

370
00:32:38,827 --> 00:32:43,250
[SPEAKER_00]: It will only correspond rightly sometimes, and you're not developing wisdom on account of it.

371
00:32:43,330 --> 00:32:49,474
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when people basically start blaming sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, for things they feel, and for the craving on account of it.

372
00:32:50,175 --> 00:32:55,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, they simply think that if they avoid certain sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, they're avoiding the craving.

373
00:32:56,279 --> 00:32:56,760
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not.

374
00:32:57,726 --> 00:33:00,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You might still well be leaning towards that internally.

375
00:33:01,009 --> 00:33:03,450
[SPEAKER_00]: Welcoming, delighting, accepting, assuming that attitude.

376
00:33:04,811 --> 00:33:11,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the middle way is recognizing where the problem is of craving.

377
00:33:12,557 --> 00:33:17,141
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that will determine whether you lean towards or lean away from it.

378
00:33:17,481 --> 00:33:22,485
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if you're caught in constant denial of any pleasure, even pleasure you don't crave for,

379
00:33:23,724 --> 00:33:25,425
[SPEAKER_00]: You're overdoing the leaning away.

380
00:33:25,605 --> 00:33:27,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not having the middle ground.

381
00:33:27,126 --> 00:33:30,787
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not seeing where the craving is, because craving can only be manifested in the middle ground.

382
00:33:34,449 --> 00:33:53,177
[SPEAKER_00]: So, understanding, then, the weariness of the sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, creates a basis for you to discern where that craving is, where the necessary condition for passion, aversion and dullness is, on account of which there is all this fire of your senses, that they're burning up.

383
00:33:55,168 --> 00:34:05,654
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would be wary of the eye, not simply by, you know, being mindful of what you're seeing, and what you're hearing, and so on, and attending to it, and try not to lose focus.

384
00:34:06,154 --> 00:34:06,774
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

385
00:34:06,854 --> 00:34:11,056
[SPEAKER_00]: How can that possibly result in you being wary of it?

386
00:34:11,076 --> 00:34:17,480
[SPEAKER_00]: You're wary of it when you start looking into its nature, discerning the nature of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

387
00:34:18,563 --> 00:34:22,045
[SPEAKER_00]: As we said before, these are things that I'm subjected to.

388
00:34:22,865 --> 00:34:24,486
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not in their control.

389
00:34:25,106 --> 00:34:25,826
[SPEAKER_00]: They're literally...

390
00:34:25,866 --> 00:34:26,827
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm oppressed by them.

391
00:34:27,127 --> 00:34:28,588
[SPEAKER_00]: My sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

392
00:34:28,868 --> 00:34:32,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot stop seeing, smelling, tasting, hearing and touching and thinking.

393
00:34:32,710 --> 00:34:33,330
[SPEAKER_00]: I cannot stop it.

394
00:34:36,211 --> 00:34:44,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do I still act and hold the view and attitude as if they're mine and in my control, when I absolutely no say in them?

395
00:34:46,257 --> 00:34:52,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start thinking like that, and you have to keep repeating those reflections, and drilling them in, and clarifying them more, and more, and more.

396
00:34:52,202 --> 00:34:53,583
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how discernment takes place.

397
00:34:54,504 --> 00:35:02,350
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you are developing the attitude of wariness towards the eye, and forms, nose, and smells, and so on.

398
00:35:04,231 --> 00:35:10,997
[SPEAKER_00]: And by being wary, you'll be unable to be careless, leaning toward, carelessly leaning away, and so on.

399
00:35:12,011 --> 00:35:16,914
[SPEAKER_00]: By being wary, as a basis, you then will be unable to crave.

400
00:35:19,175 --> 00:35:21,076
[SPEAKER_00]: By not craving, you'll be unable to suffer.

401
00:35:23,577 --> 00:35:26,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it's amidst the things that used to cause you to suffer.

402
00:35:30,601 --> 00:35:31,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And there he says it, exactly.

403
00:35:32,362 --> 00:35:34,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being wary, he becomes dispassionate.

404
00:35:35,303 --> 00:35:40,626
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being wary, no basis for craving, no basis for craving, no basis for passion.

405
00:35:42,771 --> 00:35:45,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, without passion, you really get to understand.

406
00:35:45,353 --> 00:35:53,039
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fire... the smoke's gone, because the fire stopped burning, and now you see clearly what things are.

407
00:35:53,740 --> 00:35:57,723
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the... as he says here, he becomes... he becomes dispassionate.

408
00:35:57,743 --> 00:35:59,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Dispassionate, he's liberated.

409
00:36:00,305 --> 00:36:07,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're liberated from that self-perpetuating fire that you kept providing unintentionally... kept providing fuel for.

410
00:36:09,018 --> 00:36:12,062
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with liberation, you know that you are liberated.

411
00:36:12,843 --> 00:36:14,144
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

412
00:36:14,765 --> 00:36:16,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Vimuttasmiṁ vimuttam iti.

413
00:36:16,928 --> 00:36:21,173
[SPEAKER_00]: With liberation, it is liberated, jñāna hoti, the knowledge manifests.

414
00:36:22,180 --> 00:36:23,161
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's important to know.

415
00:36:23,481 --> 00:36:25,962
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like the liberation will happen to you.

416
00:36:26,402 --> 00:36:32,245
[SPEAKER_00]: You had this special magical experience where all the fire disappeared and everything has ceased and now you're just enlightened.

417
00:36:32,345 --> 00:36:32,665
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

418
00:36:33,065 --> 00:36:50,054
[SPEAKER_00]: You know exactly that liberation came on account of this passion, that came on account of non-craving, that came on account of you discerning and drilling and training yourself in becoming wary of the sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, for which sensory strength was necessary beforehand.

419
00:36:51,442 --> 00:36:54,444
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you are liberated, you know you are liberated.

420
00:36:56,005 --> 00:36:57,266
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge is recognized.

421
00:36:57,406 --> 00:36:58,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, I am free.

422
00:36:58,567 --> 00:36:59,528
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the Arahantship.

423
00:37:00,228 --> 00:37:03,771
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not just that you are free from these things, you know what freedom is.

424
00:37:04,011 --> 00:37:06,833
[SPEAKER_00]: It is... With liberation, it is liberated.

425
00:37:06,973 --> 00:37:08,114
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows, he understands.

426
00:37:08,515 --> 00:37:13,078
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, Arahant knows that he knows, and knows how he knows as well.

427
00:37:15,350 --> 00:37:20,972
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm just emphasizing that because, again, it's deeply rooted, obviously, in all what you kind of expect.

428
00:37:21,772 --> 00:37:27,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if I just go through these motions and then this passion will occur and then enlightenment will happen and I'm fixed.

429
00:37:27,995 --> 00:37:28,135
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

430
00:37:28,255 --> 00:37:33,177
[SPEAKER_00]: It's simply work and repetitive training, drilling and enduring.

431
00:37:33,697 --> 00:37:39,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Patience, endurance, sense restraint and just reflection, reflection, reflection.

432
00:37:39,861 --> 00:37:45,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Meditation becomes then more composed form of the same reflection.

433
00:37:46,226 --> 00:37:47,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and pondering.

434
00:37:47,447 --> 00:37:58,455
[SPEAKER_00]: What is thinking and pondering then, in first jhāna, other than reflection, that is now fully withdrawn from any slightest basis of passion in regard to the sense pleasures?

435
00:38:00,460 --> 00:38:14,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, awareness will be developed on account of correct reflection, and correct reflection will be developed on account of correct instruction, and correct instruction will be developed on account of, well, correctly attending to what you hear that also happens to be right.

436
00:38:15,268 --> 00:38:16,869
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's two factors.

437
00:38:17,329 --> 00:38:26,675
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to find the right instructions, and you also need to attend to it correctly, because you might have been hearing the right instructions and disagreeing with it, or dismissing it, or not accepting it.

438
00:38:27,255 --> 00:38:28,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you haven't been attending it rightly.

439
00:38:28,596 --> 00:38:31,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And the first step of the right attention is to recognize your own position.

440
00:38:33,998 --> 00:38:35,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I know for myself what craving is?

441
00:38:36,019 --> 00:38:41,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I, on account of which, I'm unable to suffer, like a noble disciple?

442
00:38:41,261 --> 00:38:41,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I an arhant?

443
00:38:41,961 --> 00:38:42,142
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

444
00:38:42,182 --> 00:38:51,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, I mustn't dismiss any instruction that I come across, simply because of how it makes me feel.

445
00:38:52,998 --> 00:38:59,480
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time I should dismiss is after I have invested sufficient amount of effort where I understood that it's wrong and why it is wrong.

446
00:38:59,961 --> 00:39:07,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often you would see that, an attitude in yourself as well, you come across something and you just, oh no, this is wrong.

447
00:39:07,784 --> 00:39:15,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so you had that attitude, you know, denying it, but now look at it and ask yourself, do I know why is this wrong?

448
00:39:16,587 --> 00:39:20,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it simply because it displeases me or do I actually see a contradiction in it?

449
00:39:21,148 --> 00:39:22,429
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a flaw in it.

450
00:39:23,685 --> 00:39:24,386
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yes, there is a flaw.

451
00:39:24,406 --> 00:39:25,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so what is the flaw?

452
00:39:26,067 --> 00:39:27,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Clarify it, explain it to yourself.

453
00:39:28,909 --> 00:39:35,874
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you understand it, able to explain it, if you know that you know that there is a flaw, then fine, move on to the next instruction.

454
00:39:36,494 --> 00:39:48,643
[SPEAKER_00]: But more often than not, what people do is, you kind of go along, pick up these views, bump from one instruction to the other, pick up bits and pieces, never really thinking about or getting to the meaning of any of those.

455
00:39:48,723 --> 00:39:50,685
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just accumulating the pile and then

456
00:39:51,880 --> 00:39:53,761
[SPEAKER_00]: It has some sort of a shape of a view.

457
00:39:54,881 --> 00:40:03,305
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, when you come across something that's very much perceived as against the view, you simply reject it, because it displeases you, it counters you, or so it feels.

458
00:40:03,705 --> 00:40:07,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because you understood it as wrong, and to what extent it was wrong.

459
00:40:08,868 --> 00:40:10,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the most perilous.

460
00:40:10,148 --> 00:40:20,713
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of that attitude, of simply judging what instructions you will heed, an effort you make, because you don't look for the meaning behind the instruction, even the one that pleases you,

461
00:40:21,707 --> 00:40:26,709
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be able to then develop that right reflection.

462
00:40:26,909 --> 00:40:34,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Without the right reflection, you will not become weary of things, because you're not really discerning the nature you need to discern.

463
00:40:34,672 --> 00:40:37,033
[SPEAKER_00]: By not becoming weary, you will not become dispassionate.

464
00:40:38,193 --> 00:40:40,134
[SPEAKER_00]: By not being dispassionate, you will not be liberated.

465
00:40:41,254 --> 00:40:44,536
[SPEAKER_00]: By not being liberated, you will not be able to say, oh, I'm liberated.

466
00:40:46,696 --> 00:40:47,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

467
00:40:47,597 --> 00:40:48,357
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes down to

468
00:40:50,135 --> 00:40:55,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, even if you're not a sotāpanna, because this, as we said, refers to sotāpanna, it's still the same principle.

469
00:40:55,803 --> 00:40:56,765
[SPEAKER_00]: How am I feeling?

470
00:40:57,185 --> 00:40:58,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I resisting my feeling?

471
00:40:59,128 --> 00:41:01,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I rejecting these instructions that are upsetting me?

472
00:41:02,818 --> 00:41:08,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I really refusing to hear them because they're displeasing me?

473
00:41:09,602 --> 00:41:14,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it because... No, I've genuinely understood it's wrong for this reason, this reason and this reason.

474
00:41:15,025 --> 00:41:17,346
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you did understand, then you wouldn't be upset.

475
00:41:18,207 --> 00:41:22,789
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if you understand fool as a fool, you cannot be upset with that fool.

476
00:41:23,670 --> 00:41:25,891
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time you're upset with fool is when he gets to you.

477
00:41:26,311 --> 00:41:29,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And he gets to you when you don't understand why that happened.

478
00:41:30,118 --> 00:41:41,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you come across an instruction that you reject, that you say it's wrong, make sure you do it because you understood that it's wrong, not because it displeased you.

479
00:41:41,324 --> 00:41:42,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is all nonsense.

480
00:41:43,045 --> 00:41:44,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well, please explain.

481
00:41:45,766 --> 00:41:48,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Nonsense means it's contradictory, it's nonsensical.

482
00:41:48,408 --> 00:41:50,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Explain it why, where, to what extent.

483
00:41:50,809 --> 00:41:52,870
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are able to see that, clearly,

484
00:41:54,311 --> 00:41:54,831
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

485
00:41:54,851 --> 00:41:57,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can reject that and there will be no room for passion.

486
00:41:57,772 --> 00:42:02,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as we explained, reading this sutta, passion is a result of your craving.

487
00:42:03,114 --> 00:42:10,657
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're passionately rejecting this view, means your passion... basically, underneath that, you're craving against the displeasure of that view.

488
00:42:11,357 --> 00:42:13,138
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where your argument is rooted in.

489
00:42:13,718 --> 00:42:15,399
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where your attitude is rooted in.

490
00:42:16,219 --> 00:42:18,200
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a very dangerous place to be in.

491
00:42:19,165 --> 00:42:26,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you could well be rejecting the clearest, purest instruction that you could otherwise understand and become an arahant yourself.

492
00:42:27,830 --> 00:42:32,613
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm not saying you must then accept... Well, I mean, it's clear what I'm saying, but I just want to repeat it again.

493
00:42:33,033 --> 00:42:38,136
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about I mustn't reject and I must accept every instruction because I don't know which one's right.

494
00:42:38,396 --> 00:42:38,576
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

495
00:42:39,097 --> 00:42:46,221
[SPEAKER_00]: You must use your discernment, your reflection, and make sure you're not rooted in simply how it made you feel.

496
00:42:47,892 --> 00:42:51,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to get to the bottom, and you need to understand the meaning of that instruction.

497
00:42:51,913 --> 00:42:56,734
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's wrong, you need to see why it's wrong, and then you can move on.

498
00:42:58,915 --> 00:43:08,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you will not just be acting out of your feeling, which will then... which is then preventing you from getting to understand the nature of acting out of your feeling, which is what craving is.

499
00:43:10,937 --> 00:43:14,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, eh... this sort of, eh... ends up

500
00:43:16,457 --> 00:43:31,081
[SPEAKER_00]: By saying that the monks that were there, they were pleased with the Blessed One's Word, they delighted in them, positively, and while this explanation was being given, those thousand monks, through non-assuming, their mind liberated, became liberated from art flows.

501
00:43:32,462 --> 00:43:33,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly the point.

502
00:43:33,462 --> 00:43:38,383
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through non-craving, non-passion, they stopped assuming the five aggregates.

503
00:43:39,424 --> 00:43:41,384
[SPEAKER_00]: They stopped assuming control of the five aggregates.

504
00:43:41,424 --> 00:43:42,865
[SPEAKER_00]: They stopped taking them up as mind.

505
00:43:42,885 --> 00:43:44,265
[SPEAKER_00]: They stopped putting themselves first.

506
00:43:46,766 --> 00:43:54,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, upādāna has been completely dispelled in regard to the five aggregates, on account of removing the craving in how you're feeling right here, right now.

507
00:43:57,718 --> 00:44:00,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, these monks, they were listening this and became arahants.

508
00:44:00,501 --> 00:44:03,064
[SPEAKER_00]: They were obviously noble disciples.

509
00:44:04,395 --> 00:44:06,236
[SPEAKER_00]: ...of various degrees.

510
00:44:07,157 --> 00:44:26,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, not just that it immediately made sense with this, the context that was resulting on account of making sense of what the Buddha was describing, was sufficient for their minds to become fully established upon that knowledge of liberation through dispassion, through being wary of these things.

511
00:44:29,277 --> 00:44:37,544
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why I emphasize that now is because, just reading the last bit, you might just assume, oh, they just heard the Buddha and boom!

512
00:44:37,904 --> 00:44:38,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Arahantship happened.

513
00:44:39,085 --> 00:44:39,205
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

514
00:44:39,385 --> 00:44:44,309
[SPEAKER_00]: They've done all the work that the Buddha said here, in these few sentences.

515
00:44:44,329 --> 00:44:45,530
[SPEAKER_00]: They've done that beforehand.

516
00:44:47,468 --> 00:44:53,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when they were here, listening to the Buddha, they just got reminded of the context of non-craving, non-leaning towards, non-leaning away.

517
00:44:54,711 --> 00:45:00,154
[SPEAKER_00]: It just got hammered in to the point of, oh, yeah, the holy life has been lived, done what should be done.

518
00:45:00,334 --> 00:45:01,654
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't lean towards pleasure.

519
00:45:02,055 --> 00:45:03,155
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't lean towards pain.

520
00:45:04,436 --> 00:45:06,737
